        <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          It happens to everyone at some point — you step out of your car, the door clicks shut, and you realize your keys are sitting right there on the seat. Getting locked out of your car is one of the most stressful things that can happen, especially if it's late at night or you're in an unfamiliar part of Dover. Here's a clear, step-by-step guide for what to do next.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Stay Calm and Assess the Situation</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The first thing to do is take a breath. Panicking leads to poor decisions — like trying to force a window or door open, which can cause expensive damage to your vehicle. Before calling anyone, do a quick check:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Are all four doors actually locked? Try each one.</li>
          <li>Is a window slightly open? A small gap may be enough to work with.</li>
          <li>Is your trunk unlocked? Some vehicles allow you to access the cabin through the rear seats.</li>
          <li>Do you have a spare key at home or with a family member who could come to you?</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Don't Try to Break In Yourself</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We've all seen online videos showing how to open a car door with a slim jim, a shoelace, or a coat hanger. In practice, these methods almost never work on modern vehicles and they frequently cause damage — bent door frames, scratched paint, broken window seals, and triggered airbags in some cases. The cost of that damage will far exceed the cost of a professional locksmith.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Modern cars are also equipped with anti-lockout technology and tamper-resistant locks that make amateur entry attempts useless. Save yourself the headache and the repair bill.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Check Your Roadside Assistance Coverage</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you have AAA membership or roadside assistance through your car insurance, your policy may include free or discounted lockout service. Check your insurance card or call your provider's roadside number. Keep in mind that wait times through roadside programs can be anywhere from 45 minutes to over 2 hours depending on the time of day and your location in Kent County.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you need to get back on the road quickly, calling a local locksmith directly is almost always faster.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Call a Local Mobile Locksmith in Dover</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A professional mobile locksmith is equipped with the right tools to open your car without any damage, regardless of the make and model. At <a href="/services/automotive-locksmith/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">1st Capital Locksmith</a>, we serve Dover and all of Kent County, Delaware, with fast response times. Our technicians can typically be on-scene within 20–30 minutes.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We handle <a href="/services/car-lockout/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">standard car lockouts</a> as well as <a href="/services/emergency-car-lockout/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">emergency car lockouts</a> when we're available. Call (302) 735-0050 to confirm our current hours.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Stay Safe While You Wait</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          While you're waiting for help to arrive, prioritize your personal safety:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>If you're in an unsafe area or it's late at night, move to a well-lit public space nearby.</li>
          <li>Don't accept help from strangers offering to open your car for cash — this is a common setup for theft or scams.</li>
          <li>If you feel unsafe at any point, call 911. Police can assist with lockouts in emergencies.</li>
          <li>Let a family member or friend know where you are.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What to Expect When the Locksmith Arrives</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A professional locksmith will ask for proof of ownership — typically your ID and the vehicle registration — before opening the car. This is standard practice to confirm you own the vehicle. Don't be offended; it protects both you and the technician.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The actual unlock process takes just a few minutes with the right tools. There should be zero damage to your door, window, or lock mechanisms. Once you're back in, the locksmith can also make a spare key on-site if needed so you're never in this situation again.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">How to Prevent Being Locked Out in the Future</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The best cure is prevention. Here are a few habits that can save you from a repeat lockout:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Always have a spare key</strong> — keep one with a trusted family member, friend, or in your home.</li>
          <li><strong>Use a magnetic lockbox</strong> — these mount to the underside of your vehicle and hold a spare key safely.</li>
          <li><strong>Enable keyless entry</strong> — if your vehicle supports it, a key fob or app-based entry means you're never locked out.</li>
          <li><strong>Make a habit of patting your pockets</strong> before shutting the door — just like checking for your phone and wallet.</li>
        </ul>

